Britten's patchwork landscape has been  sculpted by human activity forests have  been cleared and peatlands drained to  make way for crops and livestock and  it's the same the world over the United  Nations scientist warned in a new report  that almost a quarter of greenhouse gas  is now originated from our use and abuse  of land and is no longer sustainable  according to the intergovernmental panel  on climate change 72% of the planets  ice-free surface is now used to feed  clothe and support the growing human  population the food sector is  responsible for 75% of all deforestation  and farmers now use twice as much water  and nine times as much artificial  fertilizer as they did in the 1960s  competition but agricultural land will  only get worse around the world farmers  are increasingly growing crops for  energy biofuels for cars and aircraft  but the report warns that's no dream fix  for climate change in poorer countries  in particular they compete with food  crops and that can increase the risk of  hunger we will need to see ourselves as  carbon farmers according to the report  agroforestry is one solution to climate  change  Helen browning has planted fruit and nut  trees on her pasture in time they'll  provide shelter for her cows and suck  carbon out of the air to store  underground overall you get a lot more  yield per acre if you have both tree  crops and grass or arable crops in the  same block of land the tree roots go  deep into the soil and will bring up  nutrients minerals and things that would  otherwise perhaps just be leached away  or just remain there and return those to  the soil through leaf fall  but soil is being eroded faster than its  formed even modest rainfall simulated in  this laboratory washes away bare earth  along with the carbon and vital  nutrients it contains so I think people  take soil for granted you know they  refer to it as mud or dirt or you know  very negative connotations yet it is so  fundamental to society and as again the  goods and services that we get from soil  these Somerset marshes were once  farmland now restored they lock up  carbon in the ground if we eat less meat  waste less food and free up the land  nature is part of the solution to the  climate crisis Thomas More Sky News
 

